%hazelnut 1
%title Welcome to Hazelnut
%category #6aa8f0 Basics
%% Lines starting with %% are comments and are ignored.

%chapter Getting started
%tag Basics
Hazelnut is a small, quiet place for your notes. A {b|notebook} is a single file, and it holds {b|chapters}, which you'll find in the sidebar on the left.

Select some text and use the toolbar on the right, right-click it, or use the keyboard:

  {b|Bold} — Ctrl+B
  {i|Italic} — Ctrl+I
  {u|Underline} — Ctrl+U
  {s|Strikethrough} — Ctrl+Shift+X
  Clear formatting — Ctrl+\

Text can be {fg=#ef6f6c|red}, {fg=#f0a35e|orange}, {fg=#e9cf6a|yellow}, {fg=#8fcf7a|green}, {fg=#6aa8f0|blue} or {fg=#b48ef0|purple}, and you can {bg=#574c1e|highlight} {bg=#22375a|anything} you like. Styles {b,i,u,fg=#d4a373|combine freely}.

Underlines come in {u|solid}, {u=double|double}, {u=dotted|dotted}, {u=dashed|dashed}, {u=wavy|wavy} and {u=zigzag|zig-zag} — pick one with the button under {u|U}.

With nothing selected, pick a style and just keep typing — it applies to what you write next.

%chapter Chapters
%tag Basics
Press Ctrl+T or the {b|+} next to "Chapters" to add a new one. {b|Drag} a chapter in the sidebar to reorder it, or right-click it to delete it. Ctrl+PgUp / Ctrl+PgDn flips between chapters.

Click a chapter's title at the top of the page to rename it.

Chapters can be filed under a {b|category}. Click the tag next to a chapter's title to pick or create one. Each category has a color, and the sidebar groups chapters by category. The gear next to "Chapters" lets you add, rename, recolor, reorder and delete categories.

%chapter Headings, lists and callouts
%tag Basics
Start a line with {b|#}, {b|##} or {b|###} and a space to make it a heading:

## A heading
### A smaller heading

Start a line with {b|-} and a space for a bullet list. Press Enter to add the next item, or Enter on an empty item to end the list. Indent with spaces or Tab to nest:

- Coffee
- Tea
  - Green
  - Black

Use {b|- [ ]} for a checklist, and click a box to tick it:

- [x] Write the welcome notes
- [ ] Try a checklist
- [ ] Export this notebook as a PDF

> [!blue] Start a line with {b|> [!]} and a space to make a {b|callout}: a tinted box to make something stand out. The next lines starting with {b|> [!]} continue it.
> [!] Pick its color with the callout button on the toolbar, or write it in brackets on the first line, like {b|[!green]}, {b|[!pink]} or any {b|[!#hex]} color.

> [!yellow] Lists and headings work inside callouts too:
> [!] - [ ] like this

You can also use the {b|H}, list, checklist and callout buttons at the top of the toolbar, or the right-click menu. They apply to every selected line.

To see or edit all the formatting as plain text, switch to the code view with the {b|</>} button at the bottom of the toolbar (Ctrl+/).

%chapter Saving
Ctrl+S saves, Ctrl+Shift+S saves a copy somewhere else, Ctrl+O opens another notebook. You can also drop a {i|.hzn} file onto the window.

Notebooks are plain text, so they're easy to back up, diff and sync. A bold word is written as \{b|word\}, a red one as \{fg=#ef6f6c|word\}. See {u|FORMAT.md} for the details.

Use the {b|Aa} toolbar button (Ctrl+Shift+M) for literal typing in normal mode: only newly entered characters stay literal. Existing formatting stays rendered, and literal text remains literal when the toggle is off.


Code blocks keep source code literal and add syntax colors. Use the toolbar's code block button to choose a language, or write fences like these:

```python
def greet(name):
    # Code stays literal inside this block.
    return f"Hello, {name}!"
```
